\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794403932000-engineer-cc3-fix-r7.prompt.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.muse-wonder-1.0.findings.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.prompt.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794407164000-engineer-cc3-fix-r9.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794407164000-engineer-cc3-fix-r9.muse-wonder-1.0.429-outage.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794407164000-engineer-cc3-fix-r9.prompt.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794408000000-reviewer-cc3-r8-confirm.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794408000000-reviewer-cc3-r8-confirm.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794412100000-reviewer-cc3-r7-confirm.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1794412100000-reviewer-cc3-r7-confirm.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-php/1794101833000-engineer-php-e2e-diagnosis-r2.muse-wonder-1.0-r2.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-php/1794101833000-engineer-php-e2e-diagnosis-r2.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-php/1794101833000-engineer-php-e2e-diagnosis-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/yang-php/1794166999000-engineer-php-echo-engine-regression.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log\n?? collab/yang-php/1794166999000-engineer-php-echo-engine-regression.prompt.md\n?? orchestrator-logs/1793952675-agy-gemini-3.8-flash-jolt-seat-takeover.md\n?? orchestrator-logs/1794037099-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-exception-track-orchestrator-takeover.md\n?? orchestrator-logs/1794147118-agy-gemini-3.8-flash-yin-repl-dht-refactor.md\n?? orchestrator-logs/1794252276-glm-glm-5.3-flash-yangphp-orchestrator-seat.md\n?? orchestrator-logs/1794340558-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-continuation-semantics-orchestrator-takeover.md\n?? r3-fix-wt/\n?? r3_apply.py\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 1x:xx GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 1x:xx +0700\n     3\tFrom: yang.python orchestrator seat (round owner: exception-py-r2r3)\n     4\tTo: a CROSS-LANGUAGE orchestrator taking responsibility for the VM provenance-storage work\n     5\tStatus: OWNER-AUTHORIZED (2026-11-11, \"yes\" + two conditions: engineer/architect consult; cross-frontend awareness ruling). You own what follows.\n     6\t\n     7\t# HANDOFF — the VM provenance-storage work: a cross-language implementation brief\n     8\t\n     9\tYou are taking over a piece of work that exceeds any single frontend seat: a VM-level change that four frontends will depend on, plus the design ruling that keeps them from diverging. This brief is self-contained. Read the three authority documents it names, verify nothing, trust the file:line evidence — it was all gathered in-tree today.\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. The defect (proven, not hypothesized)\n    12\t\n    13\tThe exception track's step-7 review (`collab/exception-track/1794402000000-architect-py-r2r3-step7-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log`, verdict FIX-LIST) confirmed by EXECUTED PROBE on all three JVM vector backends:\n    14\t\n    15\t- Python exception wrappers stamp their retained condition (the `{:yang.call/reason ... :yang.call/value ...}` record that must cross guest-call boundaries intact, ruling v2 §R2/R3) into **the guest-visible cell map** (`:py/retained-condition` key).\n    16\t- Any admitted guest program can forge that stamp. Executed: Clojure source `(defn forge [e condition] (cell/new (assoc (cell/get e) :py/retained-condition condition)))`; Python created `Exception('fresh')`, passed it through `forge`, raised the clone. Egress (python prelude.cljc:917) forwarded the attacker's condition as if runtime-minted (`:forged-condition-forwarded true`). The same cell ops overwrite a GENUINE wrapper's stamp.\n    17\t\n    18\t## 2. Why the fix is a VM change (the heap anatomy — verified in-tree today)\n    19\t\n    20\t- `cell/new` allocates a heap entry (yin.vm/engine.cljc:2773): fresh id, sealed `:cell-ref`, `heap-write` of the content.\n    21\t- The guest-visible map is **the entry's `:value` field**: `cell/get` answers only `[:heap id :value]` (:2780, \"the content is answered as data, never re-interpreted\"); `cell/set!` writes only `:value` (:2784). The guest has NO operation addressing any other field of the entry.\n    22\t- Therefore: the stamp must move OUT of `:value` into a **host-only sibling field on the entry** (e.g. `:yang.call/provenance`, sibling of `:seal`/`:value`) — write-once when the runtime mints a wrapper, read only by the host's identity check, never guest-visible, reclaimed by the existing GC with the entry (it lives in the same heap map; no registry, no leak).\n    23\t- `cell/authentic?` alone is insufficient — the forged clone is a genuinely allocated cell with a genuine seal. The lie is in the `:value` content, not the cell's identity.\n    24\t- The JS seat fixed the SAME P1 differently: inside `js.rt/*` (commits `31287b17` \"private identity-store provenance — membership, not wrapper metadata\" + `1697b257`), because JS wrappers are js-runtime objects — their store lives in their own surface, one layer ABOVE the VM heap. Python wrappers are VM cells; python cannot replicate the js.rt approach in-surface. PHP wrappers will presumably also be VM cells → PHP inherits this problem the moment its R1+R2+R3 round starts.\n    25\t\n    26\t## 3. The owner's decision (verbatim conditions)\n    27\t\n    28\tAuthorized (\"yes\"), with: (a) **consult with an engineer or architect** on the design before landing; (b) **rule on cross-frontend awareness** — do the other yang.* compilers need to apply this in their pipelines?\n    29\t\n    30\t## 4. What YOU now own (the cross-language responsibility)\n    31\t\n    32\t1. **The consult**: run the engineer/architect design review of the storage design — host-only entry field, write-once at wrapper mint, host-identity read, GC-with-entry, no guest-visible vocabulary unless unavoidable (if unavoidable: full `cell/authentic?` rigor — declared API change, second opinion). Route per routing-status.md; sol carried the deep review rounds well this arc.\n    33\t2. **The cross-frontend ruling** (the owner's condition (b)): recommend a SHARED primitive — one VM provenance mechanism all wrapper-minting frontends adopt — rather than three divergent schemes. The known consumers: python (now), php (at its round's start), clojure (open question: its outcome channel is internal per decision (A); whether its boundary retained wrappers need VM provenance is part of the ruling), js (already self-sufficient via js.rt — decide whether to migrate it onto the shared primitive or grandfather it; migration is NOT urgent, js's fix is sound).\n    34\t3. **The surface sequencing**: the change touches `yin.vm.engine`/`yin.vm.module` (+ python init/attachment; + php/js/clojure adoption as ruled). The yin.vm seat owns the VM surface — notify them via the surface registry's queue rule (`1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md`) and check contention before any round writes there. Designate the writing seat per round (a yin.vm-seat round for the engine field, then per-frontend adoption rounds), one writer per file+region as always.\n    35\t4. **The acceptance instrument** (already written, currently RED on all three backends — do not rewrite it, it is the contract): the three forgery pins in `test/yang/call/python_exception_test.cljc:108` — the reviewer's exact forge pair (after the fix: a fresh `:yang.call/thrown` whose payload is the clone, never the forged condition), a cell-clone-of-genuine pin, and an overwrite-attempt pin. No-regression gate: the eight python ledger fixtures + the parity pins (`be87df74`) + the shared suites.\n    36\t5. **The ledger**: append the round delta per the ledger's append-by-update rule when accepted; the py round (`exception-py-r2r3`, tip `16c6294f` + goldens `04cb7887`) is otherwise ACCEPTED and merge-held only on this fix.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 5. The constraints that bind you (so the brief is complete)\n    39\t\n    40\t- Ruling v2 (`1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md`) §R2's lifetime rules: identity-owned, outside source-writable attributes, no ambient registry, wrapper non-stacking, formatting never invokes foreign methods.\n    41\t- Decision (A) (`1794341000000`): no internal-encoding convergence — the storage primitive is shared INFRASTRUCTURE, not encoding convergence.\n    42\t- R5: nothing may route on condition-map shape; the provenance check is identity, not shape.\n    43\t- The `^:slow` gates stay; the contract test is never run locally on the laptop (owner ruling); remote acceptance runs carry the overlay-inspection rule (post-checkout `git diff --stat` must equal the intended overlay — two contamination incidents this arc).\n    44\t- The surface registry's one-writer rule and the branch registry (`exception-py-r2r3` is the python seat's; the VM round needs its own branch and its own owner — designate it).\n    45\t\n    46\t## 6. State of record when you take over\n    47\t\n    48\t- py branch `exception-py-r2r3` @ `16c6294f` (+ `04cb7887`): ACCEPTED in the ledger (24 rows green, xtrack-py2 no-overlay), merge-held on this fix.\n    49\t- js branch `exception-js-r1r3` @ `40284c15`: js5 re-acceptance in flight.\n    50\t- clojure branch `exception-r3-impl` @ `fbd8da0f`: accepted (18 rows green, xtrack-r3A4); clojure now additionally carries the owner-directed continuation-port work.\n    51\t- php round: not started; its gate was js5-green.\n    52\t- The fix round's artifacts: pins in the worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12` (branch `exception-py-r2r3`, uncommitted — the round stopped pre-code awaiting your authorization chain; commit them as the VM round's G0 or fold them into the VM round's brief, your call as the sequencing owner).\n    53\t- My seat hands off ROUND OWNERSHIP of the cross-language fix but remains the python ADOPTION owner: once the primitive lands, the python round wires its wrapper minting/reading onto it and re-runs the same gates.\n    54\t\n    55\t— yang.python orchestrator seat\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 1x:xx GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 1x:xx +0700\n     3\tFrom: yang.python seat (round owner: exception-py-r2r3)\n     4\tTo: the owner (relayed)\n     5\tRe: authorization decision — private sealed-entry storage for retained-condition provenance\n     6\t\n     7\t# OWNER BRIEF — one decision, with a recommendation\n     8\t\n     9\t## The situation in three sentences\n    10\t\n    11\tThe step-7 review confirmed (executed probe, all three JVM backends) that the py round's retained-condition provenance is forgeable: admitted Clojure source can clone a python exception cell and add the retention slot, and egress forwards the forged condition. The correct fix needs **private storage on each wrapper's sealed heap entry** — a change to `yin.vm.engine`/`yin.vm.module` plus Python initialization — which is outside the python seat's ceded surface. The fix round stopped honestly with its acceptance pins already written and red (3 tests / 12 assertions / 9 failures, reproducing the reviewer's exact forgery).\n    12\t\n    13\t## Why no smaller fix exists\n    14\t\n    15\t- Guest-visible storage (the cell's map) is forgeable by construction — that IS the confirmed defect.\n    16\t- A python-side identity map is either an ambient registry (ruling v2 §R2 forbids) or has the wrong lifetime.\n    17\t- The sealed heap entry is the only storage that is with the object, unreachable by guest cell operations, and reclaimed with it.\n    18\t- Precedent: the JS round fixed the identical P1 inside `js.rt/*` (their own runtime surface). Python's wrappers live one layer lower, in VM cells, so the same fix class needs the VM seat's surface.\n    19\t\n    20\t## The decision requested\n    21\t\n    22\t**Authorize a bounded VM change** for this fix:\n    23\t\n    24\t1. **Private per-sealed-entry storage**: write-once at wrapper creation (by the host, at the moment the runtime mints the wrapper), read only by the host's identity check, never guest-visible, reclaimed with the entry (no leak, no registry).\n    25\t2. **Scope guard**: no new guest-visible vocabulary unless strictly required; if the cell module's public surface must change, the full `cell/authentic?` rigor applies (declared API change, architect second opinion before landing).\n    26\t3. **Acceptance instrument** (already written, currently red): the reviewer's exact forgery pair (clojure `forge` + python raise → must emit a fresh `:yang.call/thrown` with the clone as payload, never the forged condition), a cell-clone-of-genuine pin, and an overwrite-attempt pin — plus the eight ledger fixtures and parity pins staying green.\n    27\t\n    28\t## Recommendation\n    29\t\n    30\t**Authorize, with the second-opinion clause.** The defect is real (executed proof), the fix class is proven (the JS round shipped its analogue), the surface change is minimal and invisible to guests, and the acceptance instrument is already in hand. Without it, the py round's R2/R3 acceptance is structurally incomplete: the retention property cannot be made authentic at any layer the python seat owns.\n    31\t\n    32\tAlso pending your word (separate, small): the P2 `type` builtin at prelude.cljc:3452 — accept it (my recommendation: it is harmless, already in the scanner goldens) and I re-scope my staged round to the remaining semantic pins, or strip it from the branch.\n    33\t\n    34\t— yang.python seat\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 01:30:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 08:30:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / deepseek-flash, continuation-semantics orchestrator seat)\n     4\tOwner decision requested: ONE question. Nothing merges until you answer or until the shiva\n     5\tcontract numbers independently prove the same thing.\n     6\t\n     7\t# OWNER DECISION — may yang.clojure's fix add a VM-level retained-cell mark?\n     8\t\n     9\t## How we got here (one paragraph)\n    10\t\n    11\tClojure R3 (protocol-condition retention through the public escape) took four engineer rounds and\n    12\tthree independent confirmations. Each round's tests were green; each reviewer found a real defect\n    13\tthe tests could not see: a token transplant (R5), then a payload-identity regression that turned a\n    14\tRound-0 green cross-language row red, then content replay/mutation plus seven observation leaks.\n    15\tA three-architect mob was convened on whether a wrapper can ever be observationally identical to\n    16\tits payload: **2/3 ruled (A)** — deliver ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads RAW (so the caught\n    17\tvalue IS the payload and every leak closes by construction), and keep the wrapper ONLY for\n    18\tqualified protocol conditions, bound to heap identity. muse-wonder (the dissenting (B) author)\n    19\timplemented (A) and returned **\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\"**; glm-5.3 (author of the (A) rationale)\n    20\tconfirmed it independently: **CONFIRMED**.\n    21\t\n    22\t## What the fix does now (`fbd8da0f`, branch `exception-r3-impl`)\n    23\t\n    24\t1. `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` — ordinary payloads raw; wrapper for protocol conditions only;\n    25\t   the per-call-site router and the witness closure DELETED. This half alone closes all seven of\n    26\t   the reviewer's observation leaks and restores the Round-0 round-trip row.\n    27\t2. `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` + `module.cljc` (+82 lines) — an issuer-side `:retained` mark on a\n    28\t   heap entry, written only by a compiler-internal `:cell/new-retained` effect published as\n    29\t   `cell/dsg__new-retained` (a name guest source cannot spell); `:cell/set!` refuses marked cells;\n    30\t   a pure `cell/retained?` query beside `cell/authentic?`.\n    31\t\n    32\t## The decision\n    33\t\n    34\tglm's adjudication, verbatim in substance: *the seam is SAFE (521 tests / 6,188 assertions green\n    35\tacross the VM seam and all lanes; no invariant breakage), but AUTHORIZATION NEEDS-OWNER — the\n    36\tmob's (A) included this seam, while the track's written authorization named only the (B) fallback.*\n    37\t\n    38\t**Question for the owner: may the cell module publish the issuer-side retained mint/query, and may\n    39\t`:cell/set!` refuse marked cells — i.e. may R3 touch yin.vm at all?**\n    40\t\n    41\t- **YES** → clojure R3 merges as `fbd8da0f` (pending the shiva contract numbers), retention is\n    42\t  identity-bound and unforgeable by construction, and the `:retained` mark becomes a documented VM\n    43\t  seam with a `state.md` heap-shape correction.\n    44\t- **NO** → the raw-payload half still lands (it is separable: revert the two wrapper forms to the\n    45\t  witness versions). Consequence: protocol-condition retention keeps the witness-but-not-identity\n    46\t  binding, so the content-replay/mutation finding from round 3 stays OPEN and must either be\n    47\t  accepted as a known limitation (with the `:forged-retention` row documenting it) or deferred to\n    48\t  a later VM round.\n    49\t- **DEFER** → I hold `fbd8da0f` unmerged, land the raw-payload half alone, and the VM seam becomes\n    50\t  a separate proposal.\n    51\t\n    52\t## Evidence trail\n    53\t\n    54\t- Mob record: `1794369000000-mob-record-r3-retention-design.md` (2/3 (A), 1/3 (B), votes converge)\n    55\t- Design: `1794366000000-architect-mob-r3-retention-design.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    56\t- Implementation verdict: `1794370000000-engineer-r3-glmsplit-implementation.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log`\n    57\t  (\"(A) DESIGN HOLDS\", 103 tests/993 assertions)\n    58\t- Independent confirmation: `1794371000000-reviewer-r3-A-design-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md`\n    59\t  (CONFIRMED; seam SAFE; authorization NEEDS-OWNER; separable)\n    60\t- Foundational finding: `1794365000000-reviewer-r3-identity-confirm.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log`\n    61\t\n    62\t---\n    63\t\n    64\t## DECIDED: **NO** (owner, 2026-11-11 16:3x +07, in-session — verbatim: \"NO\")\n    65\t\n    66\tyin.vm stays untouched. The `:retained` heap mark, `cell/dsg__new-retained` mint, `cell/retained?`\n    67\tquery, and the `:cell/set!` refusal on marked entries are NOT authorized.\n    68\t\n    69\t### Consequences recorded\n    70\t\n    71\t1. R3 lands as the **raw-payload half only**: ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads delivered raw\n    72\t   (closes all seven observation leaks; restores the round-trip row), protocol conditions retained\n    73\t   by the **witness mechanism** (c9c5533c's complete-contents closure), NOT heap identity.\n    74\t2. **Known limitation, documented here and in the ledger**: a guest that catches a protocol\n    75\t   wrapper can mutate its contents via `cell/set!` and substitute a different condition; the\n    76\t   boundary then forwards the substituted condition. Python/PHP do not have this hole (their\n    77\t   `ForeignException` is a native object with private state). This is a deliberate, owner-accepted\n    78\t   trade to keep yin.vm minimal.\n    79\t3. **R4's equivalence proof gains the decisive scenario**: does the outcome channel deliver\n    80\t   protocol-condition retention as securely as the continuation lane? The replay/mutation probes\n    81\t   (astra's battery) join the §8.10.9 scenario set. **Failure there triggers the owner's\n    82\t   pre-authorized (B) fallback** — under which the protocol carrier is expected to be a\n    83\t   closure-based immutable form (no VM change needed either).\n    84\t4. The rationale, owner's words in substance: adding VM lines to compensate for one frontend's\n    85\t   encoding inconsistency cuts against yin.vm's minimal-kernel design; the inconsistency is\n    86\t   handled at the layer that created it.\n    87\t\n    88\t---\n    89\t\n    90\t## FINAL DIRECTIVE (owner, 2026-11-11 17:0x +07, in-session) — SUPERSEDES the earlier reading of \"NO\"\n    91\t\n    92\tOwner, verbatim in substance across three messages: the \"NO\" vetoed **the divergence itself** —\n    93\t\"yang.clojure exception should converge. it should not be the exception.\" The VM seam was declined\n    94\tbecause kernel lines compensating for an encoding the owner does not want felt wrong — not because\n    95\tthe outcome encoding should be kept.\n    96\t\n    97\t**Decision of record: (B) TRIGGERS NOW.** yang.clojure's exception lowering converges onto the\n    98\tcontinuation encoding (the py/ts/php `%capture` raise-exit scheme). No VM change. R4 transforms\n    99\tfrom an equivalence proof into **port verification**; the earlier \"(A) now, (B) if R4 proof fails\"\n   100\tsequencing is superseded by this explicit directive.\n   101\t\n   102\t### The decisive argument (accepted by the owner through this exchange)\n   103\t\n   104\tReal Clojure — JVM, cljs, dart, jolt — uses dynamic unwinding with non-cooperating frames\n   105\t(throw-through-HOF works, `finally` runs from arbitrary depth), exactly the python/ts/php\n   106\tsemantics class; `recur` exists because those hosts lack TCO. yang.clojure's outcome model was a\n   107\tdivergence from **real Clojure itself**, forcing the restricted dialect (one catch, no finally,\n   108\topt-in outcomes). The port moves yang.clojure toward real Clojure semantics while yin.vm's native\n   109\tTCO + loop/recur (already compiling to true tail calls) preserves constant-depth iteration under\n   110\tany encoding.\n   111\t\n   112\t### Binding acceptance criteria (from the 2/2 depth mod, 1794396000000)\n   113\t\n   114\t1. LaTTe depth harness built FIRST; pre-port baselines recorded (the harness collects no depth today).\n   115\t2. The intra-unit private tail lane is PRESERVED in the port design (deepseek's remedy — no VM change).\n   116\t3. Uniform four-frontend probe: assert constant case only (`D(16)=D(64)=D(256)`, loop/recur +\n   117\t   handler-free chains); MEASURE the linear case at 8/32/128 × three categories; never require\n   118\t   growth contractually.\n   119\t4. Escalation thresholds to the owner BEFORE merge: any LaTTe workload >10× pre-port max depth or\n   120\t   >10,000 frames post-port.\n   121\t5. PHP's missing R1 bridge noted — \"every published entry\" becomes true for all four frontends\n   122\t   when the php round lands; the uniformity this directive asks for is then complete.\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-07 04:30:00 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 11:30:00 +07 (+0700)\n     3\tStatus: OWNER-APPROVED 2026-11-07 (\"enforce everywhere consistently. this should be a separate track.\"); v2 SUPERSEDES v1 (1794920000000) per the review round — architect gpt-6-astra, engineer mob gpt-6.1-sol + gemini-3.8-flash: all three ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS, folded below. Qwen aborted (headless tool denial); kimi quota-exhausted (429).\n     4\tReview records: collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gemini-3.8-flash.stdout.log.\n     5\t\n     6\t# RULING OF RECORD v2 — the exception track: §8.10.7 enforced uniformly in all four frontends\n     7\t\n     8\t## The decision\n     9\t\n    10\tException handling is enforced uniformly across yang.clojure, yang.typescript (js),\n    11\tyang.php, and yang.python. At every admitted guest-call boundary, an escaping source\n    12\texception compiles down to THE SAME continuation: the caller's supplied raise exit\n    13\treceiving the §8.10.7 condition\n    14\t\n    15\t    {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown, :yang.call/value original-thrown-value}\n    16\t\n    17\tand every receiving runtime implements the §8.10.7 reception rules as written. This is a\n    18\tSEPARATE TRACK: its own folder (collab/exception-track/), its own rounds, its own\n    19\tacceptance gate; the four frontend seats cede the exception-fix surfaces to the track and\n    20\treceive per-frontend results back into their records.\n    21\t\n    22\t## Scope bounds (v2: sharpened per astra §5)\n    23\t\n    24\t- \"Every boundary\" means ADMITTED GUEST-CALL boundaries. VM/custody defects and host\n    25\t  implementation failures remain operational failures (design :3904); no broad\n    26\t  host catch-and-relabel is authorized.\n    27\t- A RETURNED condition-shaped map is ordinary data; a THROWN condition-shaped map is the\n    28\t  payload of a fresh condition; shape is never evidence of an existing condition to\n    29\t  forward (the cell-ref rule, restated — R5).\n    30\t- \"Same continuation\" does NOT require identical internal lowering (R4's allowance).\n    31\t\n    32\t## The requirements (v2: amended per the review round)\n    33\t\n    34\t**R1 — the callee bridge (js AND php non-compliant today).** Every frontend's published\n    35\tfunction entry, closure entry, and higher-order entry installs the exception bridge\n    36\tBEFORE argument binding: it reads the supplied `:yang.call/raise` and delivers every\n    37\tescaping source exception AND argument-binding failure to it as the thrown condition,\n    38\texactly once, with execution after the call unreachable. js entries apply `:code` without\n    39\treading the raise exit (prelude.cljc:1581); php's function entry (:2264) and Closure entry\n    40\t(:4217) lack it, and php's raise with an empty handler stack RETURNS normally\n    41\t(prelude.cljc:194-197). The bridge must also satisfy §8.10.8: complete cleanup and\n    42\thandler/depth/frame restoration BEFORE the transfer; a later generator resume never\n    43\treuses a raise exit belonging to a completed call.\n    44\t\n    45\t**R2 — foreign reception and retained-condition lifetime (python, php non-compliant\n    46\ttoday).** On receiving a thrown condition: (1) a payload valid in the receiving runtime\n    47\tkeeps its ordinary catching and identity behavior — delivered UNCHANGED (python raising\n    48\tan already-local exception raw is compliant; python raising an OTHERWISE-FOREIGN payload\n    49\traw at :1034 is not); (2) js may deliver the otherwise-foreign payload raw; (3) python\n    50\tand php represent an otherwise-foreign payload with their own generated\n    51\t`ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original payload — catch-all\n    52\tcatches it, typed catches match the LOCAL hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names,\n    53\tand formatting it never implicitly invokes foreign methods. Retention rides the generated\n    54\texception's IDENTITY for its ENTIRE LIFETIME, outside source-writable attributes, with no\n    55\tambient registry (design :3878, :3885). Raising the whole envelope (php :2384) or an\n    56\totherwise-foreign raw payload (python :1034) is non-compliant.\n    57\t\n    58\t**R3 — protocol failures retain and forward the condition (ALL FOUR frontends; v2\n    59\twidened per astra §1 / sol §1).** `:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`,\n    60\t`:yang.call/unsupported-binding` (fully qualified) map to a documented local exception\n    61\tform retaining the complete original condition — qualified reason AND plain evidence — so\n    62\tan unchanged rethrow FORWARDS the original condition without a new wrapper. js discards\n    63\tthe condition into fresh text (:1627); python builds a generic TypeError (:1046); clojure\n    64\tretains it as catch data (:1306) but public escape reclassifies through unconditional\n    65\t`yang.call/thrown` (:1088); php constructs a fresh Error for not-callable and raises the\n    66\tenvelope otherwise (:2384), and its source rethrow rejects that envelope (:4085). All\n    67\tfour are in scope, reception AND outbound rethrow.\n    68\t\n    69\t**R4 — clojure unifies or proves equivalence.** The intra-unit outcome-token channel\n    70\t(clojure.cljc:1115, :1125-1133) may remain an optimization only with equivalence proof\n    71\tacross caught/uncaught throws, protocol rethrow, binding failures, and higher-order\n    72\tre-entry (§8.10.9's bar) — a single passing example is insufficient. Public\n    73\tprotocol-rethrow repair (R3) does not wait for this decision. Clojure's restricted catch\n    74\tsurface (single catch, no `finally`) is a source-semantics decision this ruling does not\n    75\tchange.\n    76\t\n    77\t**R5 — no new shape-routing.** Per the scope bounds above; plus: forged or\n    78\tsource-writable attributes on a wrapper-looking value never authenticate a retained\n    79\tcondition.\n    80\t\n    81\t## The baseline (audit of record)\n    82\t\n    83\tThe gpt-6.1-sol audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT, HEAD\n    84\te9a0b430) is the baseline. Citation corrections of record from the review round: js\n    85\tfollows its GUEST continuation handler stack (js.rt/ctx, prelude.cljc:1704), not the host\n    86\tengine's; php's empty-stack return is :194-197 (envelope reception :2384); clojure's\n    87\tactual private branches are :1115 and :1125-1133 (:1110 is the packet-length guard).\n    88\t\n    89\t## Acceptance gate (v2: widened per astra §4 / sol §4)\n    90\t\n    91\tThe track is DONE when ALL of the following hold, with real source on both sides of every\n    92\tleg:\n    93\t\n    94\t1. **The directed pair matrix — 12 cross-language directions** derived from the four\n    95\t   admitted frontends (clojure↔python and clojure↔php explicitly included), plus local\n    96\t   regressions.\n    97\t2. **Callable-kind coverage**: named exports AND closure/higher-order entries (php\n    98\t   Closures concretely), supported methods/callable objects per frontend capability.\n    99\t3. **The §8.10.11 exception scenarios**: binding failures; arbitrary foreign thrown\n   100\t   data; catch-all and typed catches (where the source has them — js has no typed-catch\n   101\t   syntax, clojure's admitted surface rejects `finally`; exercise cleanup in a frontend\n   102\t   that supports it and PRESERVE clojure's rejection tests); unchanged rethrow; finally\n   103\t   replacement; protocol-error rethrow; condition-shaped thrown maps staying data;\n   104\t   A→B→A propagation asserting original identity and cleanup order; the stored\n   105\t   ForeignException rethrown from another activation.\n   106\t4. **Context lifetime**: repeated success→raise→success, cross-runtime recursion,\n   107\t   suspension/resume — handler/depth/frame state restored, exactly-once\n   108\t   binding/cleanup.\n   109\t5. **Host-by-VM results**: JVM, Node, and Dart across the admitted VM paths, with\n   110\t   explicit refusals; portable parser-produced fixtures where the JVM-only kit cannot\n   111\t   reach; bundled-walker paths where admitted, never requiring unsupported linked\n   112\t   execution; bundled/linked parity and task isolation preserving existing custody\n   113\t   refusals.\n   114\t6. **Identity assertions BEFORE rendering**: original reasons, complete evidence, payload\n   115\t   identity, wrapper non-stacking, cleanup order — equal rendered text is not identity\n   116\t   evidence.\n   117\t\n   118\t## Sequencing (v2: corrected per astra §3 / sol §3 — the v1 order is REJECTED)\n   119\t\n   120\t0. Contract + focused FAILING fixtures first (retained-condition provenance; boundary\n   121\t   fixtures construct malformed invocations source cannot naturally build).\n   122\t1. **JS R1 + R3** together (R3 alone cannot cross the boundary while entries ignore the\n   123\t   exit).\n   124\t2. **PHP R1 + R2 + R3 as ONE coherent round** — function entry, Closure entry, and all\n   125\t   three call helpers (php/call, call-with-binding-profile, call-named). Landing the\n   126\t   bridge before reception repair would route local Throwables into the envelope-raising\n   127\t   receiver and break existing local typed catches.\n   128\t3. **Python R2 + R3 with the egress change** (prelude.cljc:912's always-fresh condition\n   129\t   must become condition-preserving for retained wrappers).\n   130\t4. **Clojure R3, then R4** (the optimization decision last).\n   131\tJS and Python work proceed independently once the contract lands; no Clojure-containing\n   132\tpair declares conformance before its R3 gap closes.\n   133\t\n   134\t— filed by the yang.python orchestrator seat; owner decision 2026-11-07\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
