ng axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL links past the outer context) refuse nesting the JVM parses (nested calls past 408 levels, bracket nests past 453 inside a call, unary runs past 253); time inside the bounds is not bounded by them: the pinned runtime parses a flat left-recursive chain in quadratic time (`1+1+...`: 16,000 operators 7.4 s, 100,000 about 340 s natively on macOS arm64; a 4 MiB chain hours) and an SLL decision whose lookahead walks a `*` chain grows faster (`1*1*...`: 2,000 operators 21 s, 4,000 about 490 s, refused at 4,097 links), so an under-bound source can hold the parse thread for minutes; the guards add up to about 2x on prediction-heavy shapes; a worker freed when no parse thread can be started is leaked, never destroyed on the caller's stack; the JVM oracle's own exporter overflows its default stack on a 20,000-deep tree (the comparison runs it with `-Xss1g`); a prediction whose context graph exceeds 4x the lookahead bound in distinct nodes refuses (a cap, not an undercount); measured, not gated: the backslash-joined chained assignment at 100,000 lines 89 to 150 s and the `1+` chain at 100,000 operators about 340 s (macOS arm64) | spec §7.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c named for the work budget (precondition for J6 Python peers) | P3 |\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:166:| Closed 2026-10-22: on jolt, `well-formed-utf16?` (`debruijn.cljc:253`, `debruijn_code.cljc:183`) bounded code-unit walk by `(count s)` (scalar count on Chez) causing astral strings to classify unsupported and under-counting UTF-8 byte length | glm Architect review of jolt-text-linear 1792646000000 (P2 follow-up, pre-existing in src/cljc) | yin.vm.debruijn owner with the host-boundary seat | fixed: walks