y 181,088 bytes in 3,312 allocations) | P2 |\n62-| jolt Python parser limits surviving J5: no process crash isolation (an uncatchable native fault ends the jolt process); no worker-restart claim; no cancellation of an under-bound parse (time is bounded only through the input and nesting axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL links past the outer context) refuse nesting the JVM parses (nested calls past 408 levels, bracket nests past 453 inside a call, unary runs past 253); time inside the bounds is not bounded by them: the pinned runtime parses a flat left-recursive chain in quadratic time (`1+1+...`: 16,000 operators 7.4 s, 100,000 about 340 s natively on macOS arm64; a 4 MiB chain hours) and an SLL decision whose lookahead walks a `*` chain grows faster (`1*1*...`: 2,000 operators 21 s, 4,000 about 490 s, refused at 4,097 links), so an under-bound source can hold the parse thread for minutes; the guards add up to about 2x on prediction-heavy shapes; a worker freed when no parse thread can be started is leaked, never destroyed on the caller's stack; the JVM oracle's own exporter overflows its default stack on a 20,000-deep tree (the comparison runs it with `-Xss1g`); a prediction whose context graph exceeds 4x the lookahead bound in distinct nodes refuses (a cap, not an undercount); measured, not gated: the backslash-joined chained assignment at 100,000 lines 89 to 150 s and the `1+` chain at 100,000 operators about 340 s (macOS arm64) | spec §7.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c (the work budget) implemented 2026-11-01 — the flat-chain and SLL-walk clauses above are now refused by the work axis (row 61); the other limits stand; precondition for J6 Python peers | P3 |\n63-| jolt Python parser has n