\\\\"for v in [1, 'x', NotExc, NotExc()]:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   454\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    try:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   455\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"        raise v\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   456\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    except TypeError as e:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   457\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"        print('TypeError', e.args[0])\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   458\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"try:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   459\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    raise KeyError('k')\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   460\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"except KeyError as err:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   461\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   462\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"try:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   463\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    print(err)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   464\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"except NameError:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   465\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    print('err unbound')\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   466\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"def f():\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   467\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    try:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   468\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\t                      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"        raise KeyError('k')\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_28\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All four frontends have a concrete divergence from the full contract. Clojure and Python produce the required condition for fresh escaping exceptions at their public entries; JavaScript and PHP lack that hookup. Foreign reception and unchanged protocol-error rethrow also differ. The local tests pin substantial exception behavior, but I found no real-source language pair exercising §8.10.7 propagation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_29\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The “same continuation” claim is **false in this snapshot**. Clojure and Python connect fresh escaping exceptions to the supplied raise exit; JavaScript and PHP do not. Foreign reception and protocol-error rethrow also diverge.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRead-only audit of `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12`, HEAD `e9a0b430`. No files edited; no tests run. Behavioral consequences below are static deductions from the code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**yang.clojure — DIVERGENT.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` becomes `[:yin.k/error payload]`. `try` examines that outcome and binds its payload to the handler. It accepts exactly one `Exception`/`ExceptionInfo` catch, performs no runtime class discrimination, and rejects `finally`. See [clojure.cljc:904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:904).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup:** the public entry reads `:yang.call/raise` at line 1070 and invokes it with `(yang.call/thrown payload)` at [clojure.cljc:1088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1088). Fresh public escapes therefore have the specified condition shape.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Private path:** intra-unit calls pass `[compiler-token args]` and receive outcomes directly; authenticated outcome-token calls also bypass public value/raise conversion. See [clojure.cljc:1110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1110), [clojure.cljc:1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1486). Thus its internal exception lowering is an ordinary result channel, unlike the other frontends’ handler continuations.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `compile-guest-call` supplies a capture-backed raise closure. A delivered `:yang.call/thrown` becomes an error outcome containing the **raw payload**; another reason becomes an error outcome containing the **whole condition as data**. See [clojure.cljc:1297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1297). Source catches receive that data. Rethrowing a protocol condition subsequently reaches the public entry’s unconditional `yang.call/thrown`, changing its reason rather than forwarding the original condition.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Root:** `clj/run-main` unwraps successful outcomes and leaves error outcomes as reporting values; it supplies no invocation raise exit. See [clojure/prelude.cljc:87](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:87).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**yang.typescript / JavaScript — DIVERGENT.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` calls `js/throw`; `try/catch` calls `js/try`; an enclosing `finally` calls `js/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:2165](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:2165), line 2214.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Local continuation:** `js/try` captures a guest continuation and installs it in `js.rt/ctx`; `js/throw` walks that runtime’s handler/finally stack. These are guest continuations, not host-native `try/catch`. See [prelude.cljc:1704](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1704).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for escaping source exceptions.** The published function entry directly applies `:code`; it neither reads `:yang.call/raise` nor installs a per-activation exception bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1581](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1581). An escaping throw therefore follows existing JS handlers rather than the foreign caller’s supplied exit. With no JS handler, it reaches the `:js/no-handler` expression.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `js/call` really does deliver `:yang.call/value` raw through `js/throw`. However, protocol failures become newly constructed `TypeError`s containing only selected text; the original condition is discarded. See [prelude.cljc:1627](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1627). Unchanged protocol-error rethrow cannot recover it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Root:** `js/run-main` installs a JS root handler and snapshots uncaught values; it does not repair the missing callee bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1850](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1850).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**yang.php — DIVERGENT.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Lowering:** source throws call `php/throw-value`; catches dispatch through local `php/instance-of`; unmatched catches call `php/raise`; `finally` becomes `php/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1889](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1889), line 3600. `throw-value` requires a local Throwable and then raises it. See [prelude.cljc:4084](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4084).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Local continuation:** `php/try` captures a guest handler continuation; `php/raise` walks PHP’s handler/finally stack. With an empty stack, `php/raise` **returns the error normally**. See [prelude.cljc:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:190).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for source/binding exceptions.** The function entry performs named-argument placement and applies `:code` without an exception bridge. Its supplied raise exit is used only for unsupported binding profiles. See [prelude.cljc:2264](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2264), line 2281. The Closure entry likewise ignores the supplied exit and delegates through `php/call`: [prelude.cljc:4217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4217).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `php/call`, `call-with-binding-profile`, and `call-named` convert not-callable to a fresh local `Error`; otherwise they call **`php/raise condition`**, without extracting `:yang.call/value`. See [prelude.cljc:2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384), line 4314. Even an original PHP Throwable arriving inside a valid thrown condition is therefore delivered as its envelope, not restored.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** No wrapper, local hierarchy, or identity-owned retained-condition association exists. `catch (Throwable)` receives no valid local wrapper; explicitly throwing the envelope cannot preserve the original condition.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Root:** `php/run-main` catches through PHP’s local stack and snapshots the result. See [prelude.cljc:5070](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5070).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**yang.python — DIVERGENT.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Lowering:** `raise X` calls `py/as-exception` then `py/raise`; bare rethrow uses the captured exception directly. Typed clauses use `py/exc-matches`, bare `except` selects its handler unconditionally, and `finally` becomes `py/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1664](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1664), lines 1699 and 1759.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Local continuation:** `py/try` captures and installs a guest handler, restoring saved context before calling its handler; `py/raise` runs intervening finally frames. See [prelude.cljc:294](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:294).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: present.** `py/function-entry` reads the supplied exit at line 893 and places **both argument binding and body execution** inside `py/try`. Its handler invokes that exit with the required `:yang.call/thrown`/`:yang.call/value` condition. See [prelude.cljc:886](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:886), line 912. Fresh escaping Python exceptions and binding failures follow the intended boundary path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `py/call-kw` delivers every thrown payload directly through `py/raise`, without validating whether it is a local exception or wrapping foreign data. Protocol failures become a generic `TypeError`, losing the condition. See [prelude.cljc:1034](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1034).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** Bare `except` can receive raw foreign data, but typed catches use `py/type-of` and the local hierarchy; foreign data has no generated local exception class. Explicit `raise e` applies `py/as-exception` and can replace that foreign payload with a `TypeError`. See [prelude.cljc:352](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:352), lines 754 and 802. There is no wrapper-lifetime condition association to recover in another activation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Root:** `py/run-main` catches and snapshots through Python’s local stack. See [prelude.cljc:3322](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3322).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Local coverage inventory — assertions present, not rerun.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Frontend | What its own tests pin |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Clojure | Throw/error outcomes: [clojure_test.clj:1162](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1162). Broad admitted catch, nested replacement throw, operand escape, and rejection of `finally`/`Throwable`: [line 2452](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2452). Named binding failure emits `:yang.call/thrown`: line 1429. No unchanged-rethrow identity assertion found in these cases. |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| JavaScript | Object and primitive throws, catch-all, finally on normal/return exits, string rethrow, uncaught exceptions: [e2e_test.clj:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/e2e_test.clj:254). Finally ordering/escape cleanup: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:677), line 785. Typed catch syntax is inapplicable. |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| PHP | Throwable hierarchy, typed/multi-type catches, `Throwable` catch-all, unmatched propagation, rethrow, finally/return override and exception replacement: [exceptions_test.clj:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/php/antlr/exceptions_test.clj:19), lines 33, 48 and 63. The test labelled “rethrow keeps the same object” checks its message, **not object identity**. |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Python | Typed catches across a local callee, bare rethrow, uncaught errors: [e2e_test.clj:534](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj:534), lines 554 and 609. Bare catch-all lowering: [lower_test.clj:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/lower_test.clj:197). Finally on every exit, nested cleanup, return override and replacement exception: [e2e_c1_test.clj:30](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj:30), lines 62 and 91. Binder condition shape: [call_entry_test.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/call_entry_test.cljc:76). |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Cross-language evidence: the “zero exception legs” report is confirmed for the checked-in conformance tests.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe shared kit composes donor exports and runs caller source; it contains no exception scenario itself: [conformance.clj:245](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:245). Its real-source consumers in [cross_lang_test.clj:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:26), Python’s [source_call_test.clj:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/source_call_test.clj:9), and TS/PHP’s [source_call_test.cljc:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_test.cljc:98) exercise successful calls, callbacks, identity and opaque values—not escaping exceptions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThere **is** a JavaScript reception fixture asserting payload identity after a hand-built entry invokes the supplied raise exit: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:768](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:768). That is useful helper evidence, but no second frontend compiles the throw. Likewise, shared helper tests construct conditions directly: [call_test.cljc:157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/core/call_test.cljc:157).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConsequently, no real-source language pair currently establishes the §8.10.7 propagation contract, including binding-failure propagation, foreign typed/catch-all reception, unchanged/protocol rethrow, finally replacement, A→B→A cleanup and identity, or saved `ForeignException` rethrow from another activation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Ranked divergences:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. **Missing callee exception bridges in JavaScript and PHP.** Their entries bypass the supplied raise exit for source exceptions; PHP binding failures also occur outside such a bridge. Evidence: JS prelude line 1581; PHP prelude lines 2264 and 4217.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. **Missing foreign-exception reception in Python and PHP.** Python raises raw foreign payloads; PHP raises the entire condition envelope. Neither implements `ForeignException` or its lifetime-owned condition association. Evidence: Python prelude line 1034; PHP prelude line 2384.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. **Original protocol conditions do not survive unchanged rethrow.** JS/Python discard them while constructing local errors; Clojure retains them as catch data but wraps a rethrow in a new thrown condition; PHP supplies an envelope without a valid local wrapper. Evidence: JS line 1634; Python line 1046; Clojure lines 1306 and 1088; PHP lines 2391 and 4085.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. **Clojure has a distinct internal failure channel and restricted catch/finally lowering.** Error vectors and authenticated private calls carry failures as ordinary results before public conversion. Evidence: compiler lines 904, 1110 and 1486.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nI found no synthesized engine-effect exception value, guessed foreign class-name matching, or source-map detector that forwards data merely because it looks like a condition. Dispatch on the reason of an actually delivered boundary condition is distinct from that forbidden shape inference.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Overall verdict: DIVERGENT.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"turn.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"usage\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"input_tokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1190399,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cached_input_tokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1069312,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cache_write_input_tokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"output_tokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":10086,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reasoning_output_tokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":2837}}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_17\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'content=|defn.*equal|closure.*equal|identical' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/closure.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm*.cljc | head -85; sed -n '630,677p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '1,85p' test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj; sed -n '1,95p' collab/exception-track/1794320926000-architect-dsg-condition-r4-coupling.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_17\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'content=|defn.*equal|closure.*equal|identical' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/closure.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm*.cljc | head -85; sed -n '630,677p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '1,85p' test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj; sed -n '1,95p' collab/exception-track/1794320926000-architect-dsg-condition-r4-coupling.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"rg: src/cljc/yin/vm/closure.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:438:               (identical? stored content-missing)\\\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:476:      (if (identical? stored not-found)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:13:   Results are identical on CLJ, CLJS and CLJD:\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:85:   +/- `max-safe`, else nil. The bound keeps an index identical on hosts\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:484:(defn- data-content=\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:490:  (or (identical? a b)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:493:        (cbor/content= a b))))\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:531:   ['content= [2] data-content=]\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:463:                            (when (identical? (primitive-function entry)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:494:                             (when (identical? (primitive-function candidate)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1074:   `dao.jing.cbor/content=`, the encoder's own kind-strict equality, so canonical\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1110:             :else (cbor/content= a b))))\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1122:   commits to its children's ids; structurally identical subtrees mint one\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1127:   leaves by `dao.jing.cbor/content=`), and a mismatch throws\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1938:   is no longer `identical?` to it, so a primitive added later is\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2057:                                    (and (identical?\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2062:                                         (identical?\\\\n                   (not (and (map? (first trailing)) (nil? (next trailing)))))\\\\n          (reject! \\\\\\\"Unexpected forms after fn arities\\\\\\\" {:name fn-name}))\\\\n        {:name fn-name, :meta (merge header (first trailing))\\\\n         :arities (mapv (fn [a]\\\\n                          (when-not (vector? (first a))\\\\n                            (reject! \\\\\\\"Malformed fn arity\\\\\\\" {:name fn-name}))\\\\n                          {:params (first a), :body (rest a)})\\\\n                        arities)})\\\\n\\\\n      :else\\\\n      (reject! (str \\\\\\\"Malformed \\\\\\\" operator